Give the Indy's R4600 a 256 MB DRC code cache instead of 32 MB.

IRIX 6.5 sitting IDLE at a 4Dwm desktop spends 60-76% of MAME's runtime in
mips3_device::code_compile_block -- re-JITting MIPS code that has not changed,
because the stock 32 MB cache fills and is flushed over and over. Two profiles
of the same idle login screen three minutes apart measured 61.2% and 75.9%
children, rising rather than decaying, so this is sustained thrash and not
translation warm-up.

Measured on indy_4610 -gio64_gfx xl24 at 1280x1024, -frameskip 6, idle window
(emulated t=150-300s), within-run via perf stat -I between emulated-time marks,
cycle-normalised to 2.5 GHz:

    32 MB (stock)   112.4%, 114.5%   mean 113.5%
    256 MB          130.0%, 130.7%   mean 130.4%   (+14.9%)
    1024 MB         126.7%                          (no better)

so this is not simply bigger-is-better; 256 MB is the knee. Note the profile
SHARE of code_compile_block is a noisy metric -- it swung 61%->76% on identical
config -- so the ranking above rests on the speed measurement, not the share.

Only indy_4610 is changed; the other IP24/IP22 machines keep the stock cache.
The +0x800000 slack mirrors what mips3.cpp's own constructor reserves alongside
DRC_CACHE_SIZE, so 256 MB here means the same thing 32 MB means there.

ARCHITECTURE CONSTRAINT: x86-64 only. AArch64's B instruction has a +/-128 MB
reach, so a 256 MB code cache can place two blocks further apart than a branch
can encode and MAME aborts at runtime with
    Fatal error: asmjit error 48: InvalidDisplacement: b <huge>
a few seconds into the IRIX boot (observed on Apple Silicon). x86-64's jmp
rel32 reaches +/-2 GB, which is why the lab box does not hit it. Do not apply
this patch on arm64; the 256 MB RAM patch is unaffected and still wanted.

diff --git a/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp b/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
--- a/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/sgi/indy_indigo2.cpp
@@ -405,6 +407,12 @@ void ip24_state::indy_4610(machine_config &config)
 	constexpr uint32_t system_clock = 50'000'000;
 	ip24(config, system_clock);
 	R4600BE(config, m_maincpu, 2 * system_clock);
+	// IRIX 6.5 sitting at an idle 4Dwm desktop thrashes the stock 32 MB DRC code
+	// cache: MAME spends 60-76% of its time in code_compile_block re-JITting the
+	// same code after repeated cache flushes. Raising the cache is worth ~+15% of
+	// real time at idle (112-115% -> 130% at 2.5 GHz on an Indy at 1280x1024).
+	// 1 GB measured no better than 256 MB, so this is not simply bigger-is-better.
+	m_maincpu->set_drc_cache_size(256 * 1024 * 1024 + 0x800000 + 0x10000);
 	m_maincpu->set_system_clock(system_clock);
 	m_maincpu->set_icache_size(0x4000);
 	m_maincpu->set_dcache_size(0x4000);
